cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this software, please cite it as below."
title: "fabric-physics-service: server-authoritative networked physics for one ward"
abstract: >
  A server-authoritative networked physics service for one zone of the V-Sekai
  multiplayer fabric, after Glenn Fiedler's networked physics in virtual reality
  with the authority moved to the server. A ward is a SQLite database over a VFS
  whose pages live in FoundationDB, so no client opens a database and authority
  never moves; what is left of the original design is the part that was never
  about trust, the priority accumulator that chooses which bodies a subscriber
  hears about in a tick. Bodies are stepped by MuJoCo and published as the
  100-byte integral XRGridEntityPacket, twenty times a second, within a
  1800-entity zone budget.
authors:
  - family-names: Lee
    given-names: "K. S. Ernest (iFire)"
    email: ernest.lee@chibifire.com
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0000-0003-4570-1436"
version: 0.1.0-dev.1
date-released: "2026-08-12"
license: Apache-2.0
repository-code: "https://github.com/v-sekai-multiplayer-fabric/fabric-physics-service"
keywords:
  - networked-physics
  - server-authoritative
  - multiplayer
  - mujoco
  - foundationdb
  - virtual-reality

# What this repository is built on.
#
# It is more borrowed than written, and that is worth saying somewhere a reader or a tool can
# follow rather than in prose that mentions each piece once. Every entry below is something the
# repository would not work without: the design it implements, the code it clones or vendors,
# and the specification its wire format is held to.
references:

  # ── The design ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

  - type: article
    title: "Networked Physics in Virtual Reality"
    authors:
      - family-names: Fiedler
        given-names: Glenn
    year: 2018
    url: "https://gafferongames.com/post/networked_physics_in_virtual_reality/"
    notes: >
      The design this service implements with the authority moved to the server.
      An avatar is three tracked objects, a head and two hands; cubes carry an
      authority sequence and a stronger ownership sequence. The three-object
      avatar is the entity count this repository's benchmark measures against.

